# Redirects for Cloudflare Pages. Order matters: the first matching rule
# wins, so every specific rule must come before the catch-all at the end.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEGACY URLS (301 = permanent)
#
# The previous mitez.org was a multi-file static site (/mission.html,
# /pilot.html, and so on) and those URLs are indexed by Google and linked
# from elsewhere. This React app has no such paths, so without these
# rules every old link would fall through to the catch-all below, load
# the app, match no route, and render the 404 page — losing the search
# ranking each of those URLs has already earned.
#
# 301 specifically (not 302): it tells Google the move is permanent and
# to transfer the old page's ranking signals to the new URL. A 302 says
# "temporary" and passes nothing on.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
/index.html                             /                 301
/mission.html                           /about            301
/how-it-works.html                      /how-it-works     301
/get-involved.html                      /get-involved     301
/contact.html                           /contact          301
/pilot.html                             /about            301
/free-student-support-gainesville.html  /                 301

# Extension-less twins of the above. The old site answered BOTH forms —
# a site:mitez.org search shows Google indexed the clean URLs
# (/pilot, /free-student-support-gainesville), not the .html ones. Those
# were falling through to the SPA catch-all, loading the app, matching no
# route and rendering the 404 page — with a 200 status, so Google would
# have kept them indexed as real pages showing "That page does not
# exist." Worse than a plain 404, and invisible without checking.
# /how-it-works, /get-involved and /contact need no rule here: those
# paths are real routes in the new app.
/mission                                /about            301
/pilot                                  /about            301
/free-student-support-gainesville       /                 301

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# SPA FALLBACK
#
# 200 (a rewrite), not 301. React Router owns routing on the client, but
# the server still has to answer a cold request for /about with the app
# itself. Serving index.html at that URL lets the router take over; a
# redirect would instead bounce the browser to / and lose the requested
# page.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
/*    /index.html   200
